1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:14,000
Rush Hour at King's Cross, one of the busiest underground stations.

2
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:28,001
Six different tube lines meet here.

3
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,400
along with several railway stations, with 67 million passengers passing through it each year.

4
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:49,480
The first steam railways began in 1825, and 25 years later, King's Cross had become a terminus for steam trains arriving in London.

5
00:00:50,640 --> 00:00:59,705
The railways were not permitted to expand any further into the capital, leaving 750,000 workers who arrived each day to find a way to get to work in the heart of the

6
00:00:59,706 --> 00:01:00,406
city.

7
00:01:00,407 --> 00:01:01,240
of the city.

8
00:01:01,241 --> 00:01:03,240
This inevitably led to gridlock.

9
00:01:07,240 --> 00:01:11,140
London of the 1850s was one of the world's largest cities.

10
00:01:11,141 --> 00:01:14,800
It was a massively growing industrial city and a port,

11
00:01:14,801 --> 00:01:17,940
the hub of the start of the British Empire.

12
00:01:17,941 --> 00:01:20,960
Interestingly enough, it was a very dense and crowded city.

13
00:01:20,961 --> 00:01:24,160
It was still largely built on its medieval street pattern.

14
00:01:24,161 --> 00:01:27,120
And it meant that people basically jostled together.

15
00:01:27,121 --> 00:01:29,440
People lived very close to their work.

16
00:01:29,441 --> 00:01:34,280
and it was a very crowded city full of cobbled streets, gas lights and horse-drawn vehicles.

17
00:01:35,480 --> 00:01:38,960
150 years ago, London was grinding to a halt.

18
00:01:39,540 --> 00:01:44,000
It needed an ambitious solution to link the main railway stations to the city centre.

19
00:01:44,660 --> 00:01:47,900
There was only one way to go, and that was underground.

20
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:51,160
I think it's important to remember that for most people,

21
00:01:51,320 --> 00:01:54,720
the thought of actually going underground and travelling at speed underground

22
00:01:54,721 --> 00:01:57,060
was actually a very novel concept.

23
00:01:57,061 --> 00:02:04,180
many Victorian writers saw death and doom and destruction to people who actually travelled on underground railways.

24
00:02:05,460 --> 00:02:10,360
Newspapers such as the Times actually ran quite a fierce campaign against the railway,

25
00:02:10,780 --> 00:02:15,080
saying that even if it worked technically it would never pay because people would never use it.

26
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:21,140
They were speculating about underground passages soaked with sewage drippings and infested by rats.

27
00:02:22,060 --> 00:02:27,040
One man, Charles Pearson, persuaded Londoners that they needed a transport network

28
00:02:27,060 --> 00:02:31,660
beneath the streets. He knew that if people could get in and out of the city easily,

29
00:02:31,661 --> 00:02:36,300
they could live further away and the slums in London would become a thing of the past.

30
00:02:38,380 --> 00:02:43,380
Charles Pearson was a lawyer associated with the city of London and he was the

31
00:02:43,381 --> 00:02:47,940
person who really first pressed for the building of the Metropolitan Railway,

32
00:02:47,941 --> 00:02:53,620
which was the world's first underground steam railway. And Pearson basically came up

33
00:02:53,621 --> 00:02:56,740
with the idea of digging a trench down the middle of the road,

34
00:02:57,060 --> 00:03:00,960
of roofing it over and running his railway in the bottom of the trench.

35
00:03:00,961 --> 00:03:05,860
The Metropolitan Railway Company broke ground in 1860.

36
00:03:05,861 --> 00:03:09,160
The building method used was called cut and cover.

37
00:03:09,161 --> 00:03:12,760
It involved digging tunnels which were over 20 foot wide,

38
00:03:12,761 --> 00:03:17,960
laying two tracks side by side and then bricking these over and replacing the road.

39
00:03:17,961 --> 00:03:24,260
The project was expensive, took three years to complete and caused havoc to homes and businesses.

40
00:03:24,261 --> 00:03:27,040
The new tunnel

41
00:03:27,060 --> 00:03:31,620
were very shallow running only feet below the surface as can still be seen in

42
00:03:31,621 --> 00:03:38,040
Notting Hill numbers 23 and 24 Leinster Gardens are identical to all the

43
00:03:38,041 --> 00:03:42,420
other houses in the street but their facade is merely a wall five foot thick

44
00:03:42,421 --> 00:03:49,200
complete with blacked out windows and doors they were built to fill the gap

45
00:03:49,201 --> 00:03:51,900
left in the row of houses when the line was built

46
00:03:51,901 --> 00:04:02,340
The Metropolitan Line opened in January 1863.

47
00:04:02,341 --> 00:04:08,520
It connected the three major rail terminals at Paddington, at Euston and at King's Cross with

48
00:04:08,521 --> 00:04:13,940
the centre of the city at Farrington.

49
00:04:13,941 --> 00:04:18,660
The initial suggestion by Metropolitan Railways engineer John Fowler had been to have the

50
00:04:18,661 --> 00:04:21,740
new Underground Railway running on compressed air, not so.

51
00:04:21,900 --> 00:04:22,600
steam.

52
00:04:22,601 --> 00:04:27,780
The idea was that trains would be blown through tunnels and sucked back by air

53
00:04:27,781 --> 00:04:31,304
generated in great compressors at each end of the line.

54
00:04:31,305 --> 00:04:33,355
But the system had a major flaw.

55
00:04:33,356 --> 00:04:37,520
The compressed air leaked continuously through the tunnel joints.

56
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:48,780
The Metropolitan Railway was forced to borrow steam locomotives of rolling stock from the Great Western Railway in order to start its service.

57
00:04:48,781 --> 00:04:51,880
It was a revolutionary idea and an important.

58
00:04:51,900 --> 00:04:59,223
immediate success. It carried 40,000 passengers on its first day.

59
00:04:59,224 --> 00:05:02,040
The timetable ran between

60
00:05:02,041 --> 00:05:05,385
8 a.m. and 8 p.m. with a train every 15 minutes.

61
00:05:05,386 --> 00:05:08,520
The time for the 3 and 3 quarter mile journey

62
00:05:08,521 --> 00:05:13,326
from Paddington to Farringdon was 18 minutes, stopping at all stations.

63
00:05:13,327 --> 00:05:14,680
Not much slower than

64
00:05:14,681 --> 00:05:20,940
the journey time today. This new railway had an unusual difference.

65
00:05:21,900 --> 00:05:30,080
When the Metropolitan Railway was first opened, it had been constructed with both 7-foot gauge,

66
00:05:30,200 --> 00:05:36,660
which was Brunel's Great Western Railway broad gauge, because it met end on with the Great Western Railway at the Paddington end.

67
00:05:37,320 --> 00:05:44,180
And it had a third rail added, which also created a 4'8 and a half-inch gauge track that's Stevenson gauge.

68
00:05:45,100 --> 00:05:48,540
The gauge is the distance between two running tracks.

69
00:05:49,180 --> 00:05:51,880
This odd twin gauge arrangement ended when the...

70
00:05:51,900 --> 00:05:57,560
The two companies, the Metropolitan Railway and the Great Western, fell out with each other.

71
00:05:57,561 --> 00:06:03,520
Metropolitan went back to using Stevenson's gauge, still used by most railways today.

72
00:06:03,521 --> 00:06:07,820
Without the locomotives from the Great Western Railway, the Metropolitan was forced to supply

73
00:06:07,821 --> 00:06:13,860
its own rolling stock, and for this they turned to the engineering company, Bayer Peacock.

74
00:06:13,861 --> 00:06:16,740
The engine was a 440 tank engine.

75
00:06:16,741 --> 00:06:21,780
440 is the wheel arrangement also used on the standard American engines of the time.

76
00:06:21,900 --> 00:06:25,640
And it had fairly large driving wheels as a passenger engine at the time.

77
00:06:25,720 --> 00:06:27,280
Woodham a very, very handsome beast.

78
00:06:27,660 --> 00:06:32,620
Very successful, used throughout the whole 40-year period of steam on the metropolitan railway.

79
00:06:33,280 --> 00:06:36,560
The engines weighed about 46 tonnes in running order.

80
00:06:37,160 --> 00:06:40,980
They could carry approximately 1,000 gallons of water in their side tanks,

81
00:06:40,981 --> 00:06:44,680
and the couple driving wheels were 5 foot 9 inches in diameter,

82
00:06:45,040 --> 00:06:46,960
driven by two 17-inch cylinders.

83
00:06:48,260 --> 00:06:51,880
These are the original coaches which were used in London in the 19.

84
00:06:51,900 --> 00:06:57,077
century. They have been carefully restored by a railway preservation society.

85
00:06:57,078 --> 00:06:58,220
The dreadnoughts,

86
00:06:58,221 --> 00:07:03,420
as these carriages were known, were divided according to class.

87
00:07:03,421 --> 00:07:13,340
The fares charged passengers on the Metropolitan Railway would have been according to the class

88
00:07:13,341 --> 00:07:14,322
they travelled in.

89
00:07:14,323 --> 00:07:18,140
First class would have been used by top city people traveling to their

90
00:07:18,141 --> 00:07:21,880
offices from their fashionable West London homes. Second,

91
00:07:21,900 --> 00:07:27,060
second class would have been used probably by the middle level of management, clerks and so on,

92
00:07:27,061 --> 00:07:30,180
and third class anybody else who needed to travel.

93
00:07:30,181 --> 00:07:36,400
And they would have travelled fairly uncomfortably and in very cramped conditions on wooden boards.

94
00:07:36,401 --> 00:07:42,580
The success of the Metropolitan encouraged other companies to start up, and in 1874, London's

95
00:07:42,581 --> 00:07:46,820
second Underground Railway began running along the Thames Embankment.

96
00:07:46,821 --> 00:07:50,900
This was the Metropolitan and District Railway.

97
00:07:50,901 --> 00:07:58,900
By 1884, a complete circular underground railway had been constructed as a joint venture by the Metropolitan and the District Railways.

98
00:07:58,901 --> 00:08:06,900
They called it the Circle Line, carrying passengers in its shallow tunnels just feet beneath Big Ben and the Tower of London.

99
00:08:06,901 --> 00:08:10,900
But the new century would bring a new technology.

100
00:08:10,901 --> 00:08:18,900
The use of steam locomotives on Underground Railways, particularly on the Metropolitan Railway and on the District Railway and some others,

101
00:08:18,901 --> 00:08:22,000
became increasingly problematic as time went on.

102
00:08:22,001 --> 00:08:27,300
The intensity of the service increased, the intervals between the trains shortened,

103
00:08:27,301 --> 00:08:34,100
and passengers were increasingly aggravated by the terrible pollution conditions which existed down there.

104
00:08:34,101 --> 00:08:41,500
At the same time, there were technological advances which were occurring in America, for example,

105
00:08:41,501 --> 00:08:44,500
and in Germany particularly, in electric traction.

106
00:08:44,501 --> 00:08:48,800
With electrification, the underground was able to expand,

107
00:08:48,801 --> 00:08:54,800
However, the extremely disruptive cut and cover method was no longer an option.

108
00:08:54,801 --> 00:09:00,800
London couldn't cope with the chaos to its roads and so a new type of underground construction was needed.

109
00:09:00,801 --> 00:09:04,800
This was going to take the builders deep into the London clay.

110
00:09:04,801 --> 00:09:11,800
This is a standard London underground running tunnel manufactured out of cast iron lining rings

111
00:09:11,801 --> 00:09:16,800
and this slightly larger section of tunnel that we're standing in is called a shield chamber.

112
00:09:16,801 --> 00:09:25,100
This is where the Great Head shield, the device used to help build London's underground railways, was constructed and then dismantled.

113
00:09:26,400 --> 00:09:32,800
A South African engineer called James Henry Greathead invented a thing called a tunneling shield.

114
00:09:33,300 --> 00:09:39,900
It was a kind of iron cylinder with a wall in which miners could actually work in safety and on the soft ground.

115
00:09:40,700 --> 00:09:46,780
The earth would be dug out in front of the tunneling shield and when a whole large enough had been constructed, the device could.

116
00:09:46,800 --> 00:09:48,600
could be pushed forward.

117
00:09:48,601 --> 00:09:51,300
The tunnel lining could then be erected behind the shield

118
00:09:51,301 --> 00:09:55,240
to hold the ground up, and then the process repeated.

119
00:09:55,241 --> 00:09:58,420
The Great Head Tunneling Shield enabled the London Underground

120
00:09:58,421 --> 00:10:01,980
to expand as a deep level railway network.

121
00:10:01,981 --> 00:10:04,480
The shield was the single most important engineering

122
00:10:04,481 --> 00:10:07,680
invention used to create the underground.

123
00:10:07,681 --> 00:10:10,060
London, at the end of the 19th century,

124
00:10:10,061 --> 00:10:13,540
was filled with exciting transport innovations.

125
00:10:13,541 --> 00:10:16,640
Permission had been granted to numerous railway companies who were

126
00:10:16,641 --> 00:10:19,640
proposing deep level underground routes.

127
00:10:19,641 --> 00:10:22,980
But the city and South London Railway was the first.

128
00:10:22,981 --> 00:10:29,280
In 1890, it became the first electric underground railway in the world.

129
00:10:29,281 --> 00:10:34,080
The decision to actually use electric traction was a really ambitious thing to do at the time,

130
00:10:34,081 --> 00:10:38,520
because most electric traction systems that were in use were tramways.

131
00:10:38,521 --> 00:10:43,500
Getting the electrical equipment small enough to actually run in a tunnel and powerful

132
00:10:43,501 --> 00:10:46,620
enough to move these quite large trains was a big challenge.

133
00:10:46,640 --> 00:10:52,640
The only way they could do it was to put all the electrical equipment into a small locomotive.

134
00:10:52,641 --> 00:10:55,640
The power station for the line was down at Stockwell.

135
00:10:55,641 --> 00:11:02,640
The problems they had were that the distribution of electricity from the power station up and down the line was not very good.

136
00:11:02,641 --> 00:11:06,640
So as the locomotives got further and further away from the power station

137
00:11:06,641 --> 00:11:10,640
and started to approach the station in the city up at King William Street,

138
00:11:10,641 --> 00:11:12,640
they often found that they were completely underpowered.

139
00:11:12,641 --> 00:11:15,640
And as the trains struggled to go up and down the hills, occasionally they were to go up and down the hills,

140
00:11:15,641 --> 00:11:19,360
occasionally they would grind to a halt completely and all the lights would go

141
00:11:19,361 --> 00:11:24,580
out leaving the passengers stranded in darkness the trains had technical

142
00:11:24,581 --> 00:11:29,800
problems and they were called the sardine box railway or padded cell because of

143
00:11:29,801 --> 00:11:34,360
their claustrophobic design but the city and South London Railway proved that

144
00:11:34,361 --> 00:11:43,700
electricity was the way forward for the underground another electric power

145
00:11:43,701 --> 00:11:46,419
tube company began operating in 1900.

146
00:11:46,420 --> 00:11:50,020
The Prince of Wales, Queen Victoria's son, opened

147
00:11:50,021 --> 00:11:51,994
the Central London Railway.

148
00:11:51,995 --> 00:11:56,380
The line was built entirely in tunnels, 10 feet in diameter,

149
00:11:56,381 --> 00:12:00,700
and since it ran across Central London from east to west, there was never much fear that

150
00:12:00,701 --> 00:12:05,700
it would fail from lack of passengers. Today the line is simply known as the Central

151
00:12:05,701 --> 00:12:13,680
Line and serves stations including Oxford Circus, Bond Street and Hoban. The Central

152
00:12:13,700 --> 00:12:16,780
Central London Railway was immediately popular.

153
00:12:16,781 --> 00:12:21,240
Over 14 million passengers were carried by the end of the first year.

154
00:12:21,241 --> 00:12:26,420
The system was liked because it adopted the American idea of a flat rate fare.

155
00:12:26,421 --> 00:12:31,740
It also looked across the Atlantic for its new technology.

156
00:12:31,741 --> 00:12:36,440
The original central London electric locomotives were what they were called camelbacks because

157
00:12:36,441 --> 00:12:41,180
they had a cab in the middle and two sloping bonnets on either side, which were built in America

158
00:12:41,181 --> 00:12:43,180
by the General Electric Company.

159
00:12:43,181 --> 00:12:47,780
They were shipped to England in boxes and assembled in London.

160
00:12:47,781 --> 00:12:51,560
One of them was dropped off a barge into the River Thames and had to be fished out before they

161
00:12:51,561 --> 00:12:53,680
could build it.

162
00:12:53,681 --> 00:12:59,060
The passenger cars were over 45 feet long and weighed 14 tonnes.

163
00:12:59,061 --> 00:13:04,740
Gatemen controlled iron grilled gates at each end of the cars, allowing passengers to board.

164
00:13:04,741 --> 00:13:08,240
The railway used direct current for its traction power.

165
00:13:08,241 --> 00:13:13,160
The electricity was picked up by the locomotive from a third rail in the middle of the two running

166
00:13:13,180 --> 00:13:22,105
When you want to start an electric motor driving a train, if you start it with all the power all at once, it'll either go into a skid or the motor will blow up because

167
00:13:22,106 --> 00:13:25,097
rails. it's had too much power when it's standing still.

168
00:13:25,098 --> 00:13:30,655
So you have to apply the power gradually, rather the same way as you change up in your gears in the car.

169
00:13:30,656 --> 00:13:39,473
And the way they do it is they put a grid of resistors in the circuit with the motor and the resistance reduces the amount of current getting into the motor circuit.

170
00:13:39,474 --> 00:13:43,160
And as you want to accelerate, you gradually step out the resistance.

171
00:13:43,180 --> 00:13:44,580
is one by one, you cut them out.

172
00:13:45,220 --> 00:13:47,440
And if you listen, you can hear that happening,

173
00:13:47,640 --> 00:13:50,300
click, click, click, click as the train accelerates out of the station.

174
00:13:51,600 --> 00:13:55,760
In 1900, London's population was fast approaching 7 million,

175
00:13:56,200 --> 00:13:58,020
and there was a heavy reliance on the tube

176
00:13:58,021 --> 00:14:00,340
to move people about quickly and efficiently.

177
00:14:01,040 --> 00:14:03,020
But there was a problem with the locomotives.

178
00:14:05,180 --> 00:14:07,980
The time taken to uncouple the locomotive

179
00:14:07,981 --> 00:14:09,060
when you got to the end of the line

180
00:14:09,061 --> 00:14:11,220
and find another one to put on the other end of the train

181
00:14:11,221 --> 00:14:12,300
to return, to go back,

182
00:14:12,301 --> 00:14:15,480
was taking up a lot of time and reducing the service.

183
00:14:16,380 --> 00:14:19,160
So they introduced what was called multiple unit control.

184
00:14:20,860 --> 00:14:24,740
They achieved this by putting two of the motors at one end of the train

185
00:14:24,741 --> 00:14:27,680
and two at the other underneath the passenger cars.

186
00:14:28,300 --> 00:14:32,300
The resistors and electrical equipment were put into a space above the motors.

187
00:14:32,980 --> 00:14:35,660
All the driver had to do when he wanted to change ends

188
00:14:35,661 --> 00:14:39,140
was to shut down the cab at one end, walk down the train

189
00:14:39,141 --> 00:14:41,160
and get in the cab at the other end.

190
00:14:42,300 --> 00:14:50,800
Over the next few years there was really a railway mania with many, many companies and organisations

191
00:14:50,801 --> 00:14:54,980
promoting railways, very few of which ever saw the light of day.

192
00:14:54,981 --> 00:15:01,380
But three did, forming part of today's Northern Line, Lake Alou Line and Piccadilly Line, and all

193
00:15:01,381 --> 00:15:05,580
three had been given sanctioned by the government to go ahead, but all three were running into

194
00:15:05,581 --> 00:15:06,980
financial trouble.

195
00:15:06,981 --> 00:15:12,280
And that's when the American financier Charles Tyson Yerkees came to the rescue.

196
00:15:12,300 --> 00:15:13,300
you.

197
00:15:13,301 --> 00:15:19,980
Charles Tyson-Yerkes arrived in London with his wife and several of his mistresses, leaving

198
00:15:19,981 --> 00:15:22,900
behind a shady past.

199
00:15:22,901 --> 00:15:27,160
Questionable business practices in America had led to a prison sentence for misappropriation

200
00:15:27,161 --> 00:15:28,320
of government funds.

201
00:15:28,321 --> 00:15:31,800
Charles Tyson-Yerkes is a fascinating character.

202
00:15:31,801 --> 00:15:37,020
To many people, he is the embodiment of the crooked Victorian businessman, but I think it's

203
00:15:37,021 --> 00:15:41,960
important to remember that, of course, he merely operated in the business climate of the day.

204
00:15:41,961 --> 00:15:46,920
He wasn't responsible for the first Underground Railway, but what he brought was basically

205
00:15:46,921 --> 00:15:49,520
access to several important things.

206
00:15:49,521 --> 00:15:54,480
He brought access to American money to develop the very expensive tube railways being built

207
00:15:54,481 --> 00:15:59,600
in London at the turn of the century, and he brought access to American technology, particularly

208
00:15:59,601 --> 00:16:02,480
in the electrical field.

209
00:16:02,481 --> 00:16:08,200
By 1902, Yerkes had become the chairman of the newly formed Underground Electric Railways Company

210
00:16:08,201 --> 00:16:11,600
of London Limited, with a capital of over £2 million.

211
00:16:11,960 --> 00:16:15,960
Yerke set about buying up the new electric underground lines.

212
00:16:15,961 --> 00:16:19,960
He also built a large power plant at Lott's Road, Chelsea,

213
00:16:19,961 --> 00:16:23,960
an ideal location for being supplied with coal by barge from the Thames.

214
00:16:23,961 --> 00:16:28,960
He believed that if you supplied your own power, you increased your profits.

215
00:16:28,961 --> 00:16:32,960
The Lott's Road power plant, still part of the London Skyline,

216
00:16:32,961 --> 00:16:35,960
was built to American specifications.

217
00:16:35,961 --> 00:16:38,960
It was one of the biggest generating stations in the world

218
00:16:38,961 --> 00:16:40,960
containing eight turbo generators running at one.

219
00:16:40,961 --> 00:16:45,960
running at 1,000 revs per minute, developing 65,000 horsepower.

220
00:16:49,960 --> 00:16:52,960
The opening of Yerke's first tube lines in 1906

221
00:16:52,961 --> 00:16:56,960
and the increasing electrification of his subsurface railways

222
00:16:56,961 --> 00:17:00,960
increasingly felt the death-nail of steam locomotives in use

223
00:17:00,961 --> 00:17:03,960
on the central area of London underground.

224
00:17:03,961 --> 00:17:05,960
Progressively they were forced to the suburbs

225
00:17:05,961 --> 00:17:09,960
and eventually the entire network was electrically operated.

226
00:17:09,961 --> 00:17:17,960
When the underground was first electrified, it was decided to use a four rail system, which is almost unique in the world.

227
00:17:17,961 --> 00:17:25,960
The positive one is on the outside of the rails, and then there's the one in the centre, which is the negative rail.

228
00:17:25,961 --> 00:17:32,960
That system was chosen with an insulated return, because even in those early days of electrification,

229
00:17:32,961 --> 00:17:39,960
it was realised that if the return went through the running rails and through earth, it could cause problems with corrosion of...

230
00:17:39,961 --> 00:17:45,960
water pipes and other metal in the ground, including our own tunnel segments.

231
00:17:45,961 --> 00:17:51,960
Charles Yerkes had achieved his first goal by making an efficient, electrically powered railway network.

232
00:17:51,961 --> 00:17:57,960
The system he set up at the beginning of the century is still very much in place.

233
00:17:57,961 --> 00:18:03,960
But how to give the entire underground network a uniform identity was Yerke's next challenge.

234
00:18:03,961 --> 00:18:08,960
Modern styling and improved design were the answer.

235
00:18:08,961 --> 00:18:18,460
To create a uniform identity for his company, he employed a young architect, Leslie Green, to design stations that were distinctive and conspicuous.

236
00:18:19,960 --> 00:18:28,960
The minute you see one of these stations, it says London Underground, because it uses the tiles, these maroon-glazed tiles, always the same.

237
00:18:29,960 --> 00:18:35,960
You also have all those Edwardian obsessions with kind of an English variant on Art Nouveau.

238
00:18:35,961 --> 00:18:40,380
got craftsmanship and wood and no expense spared.

239
00:18:40,381 --> 00:18:44,240
So these were lavish stations in their time.

240
00:18:44,241 --> 00:18:48,620
And they've lasted the test of time extraordinarily well.

241
00:18:48,621 --> 00:18:50,900
The minute you walk into a Leslie Green station,

242
00:18:50,901 --> 00:18:53,140
you know you're on the London Underground.

243
00:18:53,141 --> 00:18:56,220
They made something like 11 Leslie Green stations

244
00:18:56,221 --> 00:18:59,180
in two years between 1904 and 1906,

245
00:18:59,181 --> 00:19:01,240
which is probably why he died a year later,

246
00:19:01,241 --> 00:19:03,560
they say, of exhaustion age 33.

247
00:19:03,561 --> 00:19:05,940
But he left behind an extraordinary

248
00:19:05,960 --> 00:19:06,960
heritage for London.

249
00:19:06,961 --> 00:19:11,340
Yerkes didn't live to see his underground empire grow either.

250
00:19:11,341 --> 00:19:15,820
He died in December 1905 in New York.

251
00:19:15,821 --> 00:19:20,480
And the following year, Albert Stanley, another American, replaced him.

252
00:19:20,481 --> 00:19:24,480
Stanley brought even more American influence when he arrived from New Jersey and took over

253
00:19:24,481 --> 00:19:28,680
as general manager of the Underground Electric Railways Company.

254
00:19:28,681 --> 00:19:32,160
Albert Stanley made two outstanding contributions.

255
00:19:32,161 --> 00:19:34,780
Firstly, he bought the bus companies in London.

256
00:19:34,781 --> 00:19:37,340
the only mode of transport that made a profit.

257
00:19:37,341 --> 00:19:42,020
Secondly, he employed Frank Pick as his deputy in 1906.

258
00:19:42,021 --> 00:19:45,820
Pick became responsible for branding and styling on the underground.

259
00:19:45,821 --> 00:19:51,780
This included rolling stock, upholstery, lighting, architecture, and even graphic design.

260
00:19:51,781 --> 00:19:55,820
The underground, when they first opened the new tube lines,

261
00:19:55,821 --> 00:20:01,780
had the names of the station simply fired into the tiles at either end of the platform.

262
00:20:01,781 --> 00:20:04,760
One of the first changes they made so that the station

263
00:20:04,780 --> 00:20:13,780
name was distinctive was to have the name printed on an enamel plate with behind it a red

264
00:20:13,781 --> 00:20:20,780
symbol so that the lettering is on white on a blue plate with a red disc behind it.

265
00:20:20,781 --> 00:20:26,780
This became the symbol of the underground company and remains its corporate logo.

266
00:20:26,781 --> 00:20:32,780
This logo reinforced the way the three deep level tube lines were seen to be part of the same company.

267
00:20:32,781 --> 00:20:38,780
It was called the Underground for the first time in 1908, and the name stuck.

268
00:20:38,781 --> 00:20:44,780
The newly branded company also modernised its stock as the modern tube continued to take shape.

269
00:20:44,781 --> 00:20:52,780
To increase efficiency, the Underground had to find a way of getting people on and off the trains more quickly.

270
00:20:52,781 --> 00:20:58,780
They solved the problem by the 1920s by building trains which had sliding doors on the sides,

271
00:20:58,781 --> 00:21:02,980
on the sides and the tops of the doors followed the roof profile so that there was head room

272
00:21:02,981 --> 00:21:04,581
for people to get on and off.

273
00:21:04,582 --> 00:21:08,060
And this stop became known as the standard stop because so much

274
00:21:08,061 --> 00:21:12,300
of it was built that it was practically standard across all over the Yerkes tubes.

275
00:21:13,740 --> 00:21:18,740
The underground had a modern identity and modern trains below ground, but it was about

276
00:21:18,741 --> 00:21:23,386
to play a major role in the shaping of modern-day London above ground.

277
00:21:23,387 --> 00:21:24,780
The underground lines

278
00:21:24,781 --> 00:21:28,760
had expanded and in 1908 one line came to the surface.

279
00:21:28,780 --> 00:21:31,780
just to the north of London in open countryside.

280
00:21:31,781 --> 00:21:35,280
Within the next few years, an entire new community,

281
00:21:35,281 --> 00:21:36,780
Hampstead Garden suburb,

282
00:21:36,781 --> 00:21:39,780
developed around the base of the Underground Station.

283
00:21:39,781 --> 00:21:42,780
Albert Stanley and his fellow investors realized

284
00:21:42,781 --> 00:21:44,280
that there was a great profit to be made

285
00:21:44,281 --> 00:21:47,280
in the expansion and development of the London suburbs,

286
00:21:47,281 --> 00:21:50,280
as did their arch rivals the Metropolitan Railway.

287
00:21:50,281 --> 00:21:53,780
Together, the two companies expanded their networks beyond London,

288
00:21:53,781 --> 00:21:55,780
creating a new suburbia.

289
00:21:55,781 --> 00:21:58,760
The Metropolitan Railway had the smart idea of

290
00:21:58,780 --> 00:22:02,080
were actually buying up land either side of the railway

291
00:22:02,081 --> 00:22:04,880
as it was developed out into the countryside,

292
00:22:04,881 --> 00:22:09,620
and then later using that land to develop their own housing estates.

293
00:22:09,621 --> 00:22:12,620
So they got the double benefit of not only the profits

294
00:22:12,621 --> 00:22:14,540
from the houses themselves,

295
00:22:14,541 --> 00:22:17,920
but also the profits from the people who bought those houses,

296
00:22:17,921 --> 00:22:20,840
then travelling in daily into London.

297
00:22:20,841 --> 00:22:22,760
The centre of London eventually stopped

298
00:22:22,761 --> 00:22:25,680
being the place where people lived and worked.

299
00:22:25,681 --> 00:22:28,380
The rapid growth in desirable suburban housing

300
00:22:28,381 --> 00:22:33,420
meant that the city centre was reinventing itself as a place to visit for entertainment and shopping.

301
00:22:34,620 --> 00:22:37,780
Very early on, the people who run the London transport system

302
00:22:37,781 --> 00:22:42,180
realised that what they were selling was not a transport system, but a city.

303
00:22:43,180 --> 00:22:46,600
See, if you're selling tickets on the tube, you don't say, come on the tube, it's great fun.

304
00:22:47,020 --> 00:22:49,820
You say, go to the theatre, it's great fun, and go by tube.

305
00:22:50,120 --> 00:22:52,200
Or you say go shopping in the department stores.

306
00:22:52,440 --> 00:22:53,660
It's great, go by tube.

307
00:22:55,980 --> 00:22:58,360
The underground network expanded from...

308
00:22:58,380 --> 00:23:03,100
the centre of London and out to the suburbs, reaching ever more people.

309
00:23:03,101 --> 00:23:06,940
But to make a profit, it needed even more passengers.

310
00:23:06,941 --> 00:23:12,300
Frank Pick, the assistant manager of the Underground, hit on a radical idea, a poster campaign

311
00:23:12,301 --> 00:23:17,260
to promote the tube, not just as a way of getting to work, but for leisure activities.

312
00:23:17,261 --> 00:23:23,740
By the 1920s and 30s, the Underground was producing a really large number of posters,

313
00:23:23,741 --> 00:23:28,300
and they had easily fulfilled their function of just publicising the system and the system.

314
00:23:28,380 --> 00:23:30,840
getting people to use the underground.

315
00:23:30,841 --> 00:23:32,380
And they became much more than that.

316
00:23:32,381 --> 00:23:36,100
They became a way of actually promoting London.

317
00:23:37,220 --> 00:23:39,700
So they've sold the West End as the place to shop,

318
00:23:39,701 --> 00:23:41,260
the place to play, the place to drink,

319
00:23:41,261 --> 00:23:43,820
the place to flirt, the place to do all of those things.

320
00:23:43,821 --> 00:23:46,060
Consciously, posters saying,

321
00:23:46,061 --> 00:23:47,740
come to the West End, it's great.

322
00:23:47,741 --> 00:23:50,200
Go to the theatre, it's wonderful.

323
00:23:50,201 --> 00:23:51,820
Go to Selfridges and spend your money

324
00:23:51,821 --> 00:23:53,720
and buy a two-bobb ticket.

325
00:23:53,721 --> 00:23:55,060
That's what they sold.

326
00:23:55,061 --> 00:23:56,900
And we bought it.

327
00:23:56,901 --> 00:23:58,360
The marketing and poster campaign,

328
00:23:58,380 --> 00:24:02,380
exemplify the forward-thinking and innovation of the underground.

329
00:24:02,381 --> 00:24:06,380
And in the engineering arena, older technology was still serving them well.

330
00:24:06,381 --> 00:24:09,380
The original braking system that was used on London Underground

331
00:24:09,381 --> 00:24:13,380
was designed by George Westinghouse of America in the 1870s.

332
00:24:13,381 --> 00:24:16,380
It consisted basically of compressed air supply,

333
00:24:16,381 --> 00:24:19,380
which provided you with a constant supply of air,

334
00:24:19,381 --> 00:24:23,380
and the air was to use in the brake cylinders,

335
00:24:23,381 --> 00:24:27,380
and the brake cylinders were used to push a block against the wheel

336
00:24:27,381 --> 00:24:29,380
to slow it down during braking.

337
00:24:29,381 --> 00:24:31,380
On each car a thing called a triple valve.

338
00:24:31,381 --> 00:24:34,380
And the triple valve recharged the brakes,

339
00:24:34,381 --> 00:24:36,380
released the brakes, and applied the brakes.

340
00:24:36,381 --> 00:24:38,380
So it had three functions.

341
00:24:38,381 --> 00:24:41,380
Technical expertise and simplicity

342
00:24:41,381 --> 00:24:43,380
were the watchwords of the London Underground.

343
00:24:43,381 --> 00:24:46,380
Simplicity exemplified by Frank Pick

344
00:24:46,381 --> 00:24:48,380
and his hand-chosen architects.

345
00:24:48,381 --> 00:24:50,380
In 1923,

346
00:24:50,381 --> 00:24:52,380
Pick employed Charles Henry Holden

347
00:24:52,381 --> 00:24:53,380
to design the stations

348
00:24:53,381 --> 00:24:56,380
on the City and South London Railway Extension.

349
00:24:56,381 --> 00:25:01,380
Haldon went on to create a house style that rationalised the often chaotic layout

350
00:25:01,381 --> 00:25:06,380
and provided a much more modern façade, still seen in many underground stations.

351
00:25:06,381 --> 00:25:11,380
The design was a very clever one based on simple geometric shapes

352
00:25:11,381 --> 00:25:15,380
and using a modern material and concrete for the roofs,

353
00:25:15,381 --> 00:25:21,380
but using traditional brickwork designs for the main body of the building

354
00:25:21,381 --> 00:25:26,360
and creating what now in retrospect is some of the best modern

355
00:25:26,380 --> 00:25:31,340
building designs in London and perhaps in the rest of Britain as well.

356
00:25:31,341 --> 00:25:35,260
But in one key area the underground was far from simple.

357
00:25:35,261 --> 00:25:39,980
As the network expanded it was becoming increasingly difficult to follow and

358
00:25:39,981 --> 00:25:44,980
understand the London underground map. An alternative was needed. And so in

359
00:25:44,981 --> 00:25:49,900
1931 draftsman Harry Beck produced the first diagrammatic map.

360
00:25:53,900 --> 00:25:56,220
His solution was radical.

361
00:25:56,380 --> 00:26:02,780
He redesigned the map as an electrical circuit, abandoning geographical truth for the sake of simplicity.

362
00:26:05,380 --> 00:26:12,180
The central areas of the map, which contain the most complicated interchanges, were enlarged in relation to the outer areas.

363
00:26:12,940 --> 00:26:19,760
Initially, London Underground rejected the design because it was too revolutionary, and because it had no bearing on the real geography.

364
00:26:20,380 --> 00:26:26,300
They eventually published it in 1933, and it was greeted with enthusiasm for being clear,

365
00:26:26,380 --> 00:26:31,060
and simple to use. The diagrammatic map is now a standard means of

366
00:26:31,061 --> 00:26:34,200
representing transport systems all over the world.

367
00:26:34,201 --> 00:26:42,920
By the 1930s, control of all the original tube companies was taken over by the

368
00:26:42,921 --> 00:26:45,700
newly formed London passenger transport board.

369
00:26:45,701 --> 00:26:49,780
Its role was to oversee the continued expansion of the underground.

370
00:26:49,781 --> 00:26:53,780
With the population of Greater London approaching nine million,

371
00:26:53,781 --> 00:26:56,360
something had to be done to increase the flow of power

372
00:26:56,380 --> 00:26:58,295
passengers through the system.

373
00:26:58,296 --> 00:27:02,380
It wasn't feasible to increase the tunnel size for wider trains.

374
00:27:02,381 --> 00:27:06,380
So in 1935 the cars were redesigned to accommodate more people.

375
00:27:06,381 --> 00:27:13,380
One of the major problems of all the pre-1935 trains built for London Underground

376
00:27:13,381 --> 00:27:16,380
was that you had the switch compartment behind the driver

377
00:27:16,381 --> 00:27:20,380
and that used up 15% of the available passenger space.

378
00:27:20,381 --> 00:27:26,360
By 1935 they had sufficient design progress to put most

379
00:27:26,380 --> 00:27:27,500
of the equipment below the floor.

380
00:27:28,780 --> 00:27:31,320
Motors were smaller, the compressors were smaller,

381
00:27:31,840 --> 00:27:33,660
all the power supply systems were smaller.

382
00:27:33,940 --> 00:27:36,380
And they produced these prototype trains in 1935.

383
00:27:38,140 --> 00:27:40,560
Since its opening in 1863,

384
00:27:41,100 --> 00:27:43,360
the London Underground has played a vital role

385
00:27:43,361 --> 00:27:44,700
in the lives of Londoners,

386
00:27:45,120 --> 00:27:47,420
not least during the Second World War.

387
00:27:50,480 --> 00:27:55,140
During the Blitz, 79 tube stations were used as air raid shelters,

388
00:27:55,141 --> 00:27:58,140
accommodating thousands of people every night.

389
00:27:58,141 --> 00:28:08,140
The 130,000 people

390
00:28:08,141 --> 00:28:11,140
raise a good many problems.

391
00:28:11,141 --> 00:28:15,140
They get there early and so need something to eat long before bedtime.

392
00:28:15,141 --> 00:28:19,140
London Transport has established six depots where the food is received

393
00:28:19,141 --> 00:28:22,140
and prepared for dispatch to the station canteens.

394
00:28:22,141 --> 00:28:24,140
The trains carried the women of the undergrine.

395
00:28:24,141 --> 00:28:28,580
the Women of the Underground Catering Service who provided refreshments for the thousands

396
00:28:28,581 --> 00:28:33,420
of Londoners who sheltered on the platforms on countless nights during the war.

397
00:28:33,421 --> 00:28:40,380
The London Underground had one of the most efficient catering establishments.

398
00:28:40,381 --> 00:28:47,760
In fact there was a total of over 75 which the catering department looked after and so they took

399
00:28:47,761 --> 00:28:53,020
on the job of providing refreshment trains.

400
00:28:53,021 --> 00:28:56,020
Buns, chocolates, timetables.

401
00:28:56,021 --> 00:29:07,020
Now these refreshment trains ran roughly about twice a day, once late evening, and once before the service started in the morning.

402
00:29:07,021 --> 00:29:09,255
Tons of food are consumed every night.

403
00:29:09,256 --> 00:29:13,020
To prepare and deliver it is the work of over a thousand people.

404
00:29:13,021 --> 00:29:21,020
As well as looking after the needs of the people sheltering in the tube, 17,000 women took on jobs in the underground.

405
00:29:21,021 --> 00:29:22,020
They created a thriving underground world.

406
00:29:22,021 --> 00:29:28,020
underground world, producing weapons in the munitions and armaments factories beneath the city streets.

407
00:29:28,021 --> 00:29:34,020
During the Second World War, large numbers of women came to be employed by the underground group,

408
00:29:34,021 --> 00:29:38,020
mostly in place of men, of course, who had gone off to fight in the armed forces.

409
00:29:38,021 --> 00:29:45,020
But they took on a variety of roles, not just working in stations, but also in engineering capacities,

410
00:29:45,021 --> 00:29:51,020
such as relaying track or working in the underground factories, building new rolling stock and repairing trains.

411
00:29:51,021 --> 00:29:51,721
trains.

412
00:29:51,722 --> 00:29:58,020
Millions of vital bomber, fighter and tank parts poured out from this underground

413
00:30:00,860 --> 00:30:06,020
arsenal to the allied forces in all corners of the world.

414
00:30:08,020 --> 00:30:13,047
The underground was also important for the wartime prime minister, Winston Churchill.

415
00:30:13,048 --> 00:30:19,020
The deep tunnels beneath downstreet station became the subterranean headquarters for the war cabinet.

416
00:30:19,021 --> 00:30:28,020
This passageway would have been the way out of the station up until 1932, but it had a much more important role during the war years.

417
00:30:28,021 --> 00:30:33,020
The Railway Executive Committee used this area for their meeting room,

418
00:30:33,021 --> 00:30:40,020
and it's where some of Britain's most important decisions for rail movements, troop movements during the war years were taken.

419
00:30:40,021 --> 00:30:43,020
But it also had another important use.

420
00:30:43,021 --> 00:30:49,020
Up until 1942 when the war cabinet rooms were completed, Winston Churchill came to the war,

421
00:30:49,021 --> 00:30:55,520
here and met with some of the Joint Chiefs of Staff and with the War Cabinet.

422
00:30:55,521 --> 00:31:03,340
Here at track level, the platform areas were converted for use as a telephone exchange, as kitchens,

423
00:31:03,341 --> 00:31:08,740
as bedrooms and offices, and what we're standing in was one of the dining rooms.

424
00:31:08,741 --> 00:31:13,260
There were two dining rooms here and we can tell that this one was one of the executives'

425
00:31:13,261 --> 00:31:19,000
dining rooms and that was because they had wallpaper on the walls and a bell push to some

426
00:31:19,020 --> 00:31:20,020
summon the steward.

427
00:31:20,021 --> 00:31:29,020
That gives you an idea of what kind of noise must have been present while people were

428
00:31:29,021 --> 00:31:35,260
trying to work, eat and even sleep as trains were passing by the site not more than six

429
00:31:35,261 --> 00:31:39,660
feet away from where we're standing.

430
00:31:39,661 --> 00:31:46,940
Between August 1940 and July 1941, nearly 40,000 bombs and millions of incendiaries fell on

431
00:31:46,941 --> 00:31:52,460
London. Thousands of underground carriages were damaged or destroyed but worse

432
00:31:52,461 --> 00:31:55,580
than the damage to the rolling stop and infrastructure of the underground

433
00:31:55,581 --> 00:31:58,180
several disasters befell the tube.

434
00:32:02,180 --> 00:32:07,160
Probably one of the worst was at Bank Station which is right in the heart of

435
00:32:07,161 --> 00:32:12,140
Financial London where a German bomb penetrated the ticket office roof and slid

436
00:32:12,141 --> 00:32:16,380
down the escalator shafts before exploding and it took over 18 months to

437
00:32:16,381 --> 00:32:19,940
reconstruct the station from the devastation caused.

438
00:32:19,941 --> 00:32:23,420
56 people were killed in this disaster.

439
00:32:23,421 --> 00:32:28,060
In 1940, South London's Ballam station was also destroyed by a German bomb,

440
00:32:28,061 --> 00:32:32,140
which burst a water main, drowning 68 people who were sheltering there.

441
00:32:32,141 --> 00:32:36,820
One of the worst civilian disasters during the Second World War took place at a partially

442
00:32:36,821 --> 00:32:40,860
built underground station at Bethnal Green in East London.

443
00:32:40,861 --> 00:32:46,360
Following a false air raid warning, a woman and child tripped halfway down the staircases.

444
00:32:46,380 --> 00:32:49,820
and in the ensuing crush nearly 200 people were killed.

445
00:32:51,060 --> 00:32:53,080
This tragedy was so awful,

446
00:32:53,500 --> 00:32:57,040
the British government kept the details secret for nearly two years.

447
00:32:58,820 --> 00:33:03,560
After the war, the underground required an enormous quantity of new rolling stock

448
00:33:03,561 --> 00:33:05,260
to replace what had been destroyed.

449
00:33:06,520 --> 00:33:10,520
The new trains took to the tracks on the district line in 1953,

450
00:33:11,160 --> 00:33:13,640
just as the last of the wooden cars were scrapped.

451
00:33:14,140 --> 00:33:16,360
They were inspired by wartime technology.

452
00:33:16,380 --> 00:33:26,380
Following the experience with aluminium aircraft construction, London Underground used that experience to develop an aluminium car body.

453
00:33:26,381 --> 00:33:34,380
Aluminium was a good material to use because it was 20% lighter than steel, it didn't rust, and you could also get away with not painting it.

454
00:33:34,381 --> 00:33:39,380
In fact, you could save two tonnes of paint per vehicle if you didn't paint it.

455
00:33:39,381 --> 00:33:45,380
So in 1953 they tried running an unpainted vehicle around the system and it was soon accepted by the public.

456
00:33:45,381 --> 00:33:46,940
soon accepted by the public.

457
00:33:46,941 --> 00:33:52,620
So from that day onwards, all London Underground trains were built of unpainted aluminium.

458
00:33:52,621 --> 00:33:58,500
And only since the mid-1980s when we were hit by the scourge of graffiti,

459
00:33:58,501 --> 00:34:03,500
did London Underground start having to repaint vehicles.

460
